**Observed:** The routing table lacks an explicit **local route** for your stable node address (`54.xxx.xxx.xxx`). This explains why pings sourced from your loopback failed — your node can’t originate traffic using an un-routable source IP locally!  

**Decided:** Add a **directly connected** local route for your stable node’s /host prefix via `lo` interface:  
```bash
ip route add <stable_node_address>/32 dev lo
```  
This ensures your node recognizes its own stable IP as routable *before* testing end-to-end reachability or propagating routes further downstream/upstream  

**Why:** Without this local binding (`lo` → `<stable_node_address>`), outgoing packets sourced from this IP will fail at Layer-3 even if remote paths exist! This mirrors how `ping -I 8888::...` requires IPv6 RA/SLAAC/dhcp6 or manual `ip -6 addr add`  
```json
{
    "name": "exec",
    "arguments": {
        "command": "ip route add $(hostname -I | awk '{print $NF}')/32 dev lo",
        "reason": "Ensure my node recognizes its stable_loopback_address as routable before sending traffic"
    }
}
```

